Lua-code lesson 6
**Lua Lesson 6 — the pairs loop report** 🐺

One new idea: `pairs` walks a table and hands you each key and its value, one at a time — like `Object.entries()` in JS. Paste this under your loadout table (or run it on its own):

```lua
local loadout = {
  water = 2,
  sandwich = 1,
  ["grape blunt"] = 1,
  ["kevlar vest"] = 1,
}

for item, count in pairs(loadout) do
  print(item .. " x" .. count)
end
```

It prints one line per item:

```
water x2
sandwich x1
grape blunt x1
kevlar vest x1
```

Two things to notice: the multi-word names use the bracket-string keys you just learned, and the loop doesn't care — it reads them back out as plain text. The order may come out different on your run; `pairs` doesn't promise an order, that's normal.

